// The source frames don't cover equal visual distance from one to the next —
// like a real pendulum, the swish naturally slows near the two ends of its arc
// and moves fastest through the middle. An even time-per-frame split (Motion's
// default for a plain array of keyframes) gives the slow segments near each
// extreme the same time slice as the fast middle ones, which reads as the
// whole loop visibly pausing at both ends. These are each segment's own share
// of the total distance traveled (computed post-densification, above),
// normalized to cumulative fractions of the total (0 to 1) — pass as
// `transition.times` alongside the `d` keyframes so time is spent proportional
// to how far the tail is actually moving, not a fixed 1/32nd share each.
